THEORETICAL BACKGROUND
Blade Element Momentum Theory (BEMT)
Blade Element Momentum Theory (BEMT) combines momentum theory and blade element theory to estimate the aerodynamic forces on rotors by discretizing each blade into several elements and applying local flow assumptions. The local thrust and torque are computed based on sectional aerodynamic properties, such as airfoil lift and drag coefficients, and integrated over the blade span. BEMT offers high computational efficiency, making it widely used for preliminary design, performance estimation, and simulation of rotorcraft and propeller systems (Ref.5). However, BEMT assumes a simplified, steady inflow model and often uses uniform inflow, which can limit accuracy.
